    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Zu Chongzhi was one of the greatest mathematicians in ancient China. He was born during the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Northern and Southern dynasties",
            "zh": "南北朝"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". His family was very talented. They had been studying mathematics and "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "astronomy",
            "ref": "v-astronomy"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " for generations. From a young age, Zu was taught natural science, astronomy, maths, "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "philosophy",
            "ref": "v-philosophy"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", and so on. The little boy was interested in all of these subjects, especially in maths."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Zu was best known for his calculation of pi. According to historical records, he did all the work using only wooden sticks. It took him lots of time to work out the value—between 3.1415926 and 3.1415927. No one at the time could calculate it more "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "accurately",
            "ref": "v-accurately"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " than Zu. The achievement has been recognised worldwide. To remember Zu, some mathematicians have suggested calling pi “"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Zu Lv",
            "zh": "祖率"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "”."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Zu was also known for working out how to calculate the "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "volume",
            "ref": "v-volume"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of a "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "sphere",
            "ref": "v-sphere"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". He did this with the help of his son "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Zu Gengzhi",
            "zh": "祖暅之"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". The two recorded their achievement in "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "Zhui Shu"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "",
            "zh": "《缀术》"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". The book was used as a maths textbook during the Tang dynasty."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "But Zu was successful not only in maths; he also did well in astronomy. He worked out that a year should be 365.24281481 days long and created the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Daming Calendar",
            "zh": "大明历"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". However, government officials at that time did not agree with Zu. The great mathematician never saw his calendar put into use. After his death, Zu Gengzhi continued to prove that the Daming Calendar was more accurate. Almost ten years later, the new calendar was finally accepted."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Zu was also an inventor. He once made a "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "vehicle",
            "zh": "工具"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " that carried a "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "pointer",
            "zh": "指针"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". However, the vehicle turned, and the pointer always pointed to the South. Zu also invented the 1,000-li Boat. The boat could travel 100 li (about 60 kilometres) in a day."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
